846
00:48:23,866 --> 00:48:26,100
So, let me do a quick—

847
00:48:26,100 --> 00:48:29,233
kind of like a quick rundown on UV maps.

848
00:48:29,333 --> 00:48:33,000
Actually let me ask you,
do you guys know what UV mapping is?

849
00:48:34,100 --> 00:48:36,033
Sound off in the chat.

850
00:48:36,033 --> 00:48:38,866
Do you know what it's all about?

851
00:48:38,866 --> 00:48:41,300
Like, why is it so important?

852
00:48:48,900 --> 00:48:50,733
That chocolate keeps

853
00:48:50,733 --> 00:48:53,733
showing up, I want chocolate now!
<i>[Laughs]</i>

854
00:48:55,800 --> 00:48:57,900
Chocolate!

855
00:48:57,900 --> 00:49:00,200
If you know me,

856
00:49:00,200 --> 00:49:03,166
you know I'm crazy
when it comes to chocolate.

857
00:49:03,166 --> 00:49:06,166
I love chocolate.

858
00:49:06,633 --> 00:49:09,633
<i>Chocolates. </i>

859
00:49:12,966 --> 00:49:13,600
Sound off.

860
00:49:13,600 --> 00:49:16,600
Does anybody know what UV wrapping?

861
00:49:28,300 --> 00:49:29,900
I keep seeing that chocolate.

862
00:49:29,900 --> 00:49:33,066
—to make textures
go around the model properly.

863
00:49:33,166 --> 00:49:36,166
Yeah.

864
00:49:37,200 --> 00:49:37,833
You're mapping

865
00:49:37,833 --> 00:49:42,100
polygon squares in this case to a texture.

866
00:49:42,100 --> 00:49:45,100
You're deciding which square goes where.

867
00:49:48,466 --> 00:49:50,766
All right.

868
00:49:50,766 --> 00:49:53,300
Yeah, that's pretty much it.

869
00:49:53,300 --> 00:49:56,233
You're basically taking a 3D model

870
00:49:56,233 --> 00:50:00,900
and you're telling blender, “Okay,
let's make this 2D so that I can paint it.”

871
00:50:01,600 --> 00:50:04,600
So, for example, if you were to take—

872
00:50:04,700 --> 00:50:08,300
I always like to use this example,
a cereal box

873
00:50:08,766 --> 00:50:12,266
Because that was actually
my favorite thing to do as a kid

874
00:50:12,800 --> 00:50:16,133
was after you're done with the cereal,
there's

875
00:50:16,133 --> 00:50:19,500
always a lot of games in the back
you could cut out and like

876
00:50:20,166 --> 00:50:23,166
draw on it, or characters to cut out.

877
00:50:23,700 --> 00:50:27,433
But, let's think
about the box itself, right?

878
00:50:29,133 --> 00:50:31,866
The box has corners

879
00:50:31,866 --> 00:50:34,333
and it has flaps,

880
00:50:34,333 --> 00:50:35,900
right?

881
00:50:35,900 --> 00:50:38,733
So let's say
you were to take a big pair of scissors

882
00:50:38,733 --> 00:50:42,166
and you were to cut the box
at the corners.

883
00:50:42,833 --> 00:50:44,333
<i>[Vocalizing]</i>

884
00:50:44,333 --> 00:50:47,333
And then the bottom flaps,
you open them up.

885
00:50:47,400 --> 00:50:48,500
Now what happens?

886
00:50:48,500 --> 00:50:52,433
You're going to take that box,
and you're going to lay it on the table.

887
00:50:52,833 --> 00:50:56,400
Now, that once was a 3D

888
00:50:56,400 --> 00:51:01,866
object is now a fully-flat 2D object
to play around with.

889
00:51:02,566 --> 00:51:04,400
And that's basically what we're doing.

890
00:51:04,400 --> 00:51:07,700
We're just basically taking that
and making it flat.

891
00:51:08,100 --> 00:51:11,766
Another great example
is anybody

892
00:51:11,766 --> 00:51:14,766
who likes to sew—

893
00:51:15,500 --> 00:51:19,766
Basically, kind of like, the sewing pattern.

894
00:51:19,966 --> 00:51:24,666
So, if you were to take a sleeve
and cut it by the seams,

895
00:51:25,133 --> 00:51:28,133
you can lay it flat
and you can see the pattern

896
00:51:28,666 --> 00:51:30,900
of that sleeve, you know?

897
00:51:30,900 --> 00:51:33,900
And so, it's basically the same concept.

898
00:51:34,000 --> 00:51:35,866
So, let's get to it.

